  "notes": [
    "Port 0 is open drain with no internal pull-ups. To use it as ordinary output you need an external pull-up resistor on every bit, typically a 10k resistor network. Ports 1, 2 and 3 have internal pull-ups and do not.",
    "EA must be tied HIGH to run from internal flash. Tied low the chip ignores its own memory and fetches from an external ROM. Floating is why a freshly wired 8051 does nothing at all.",
    "RESET is ACTIVE HIGH, which is backwards from nearly every other microcontroller. The standard circuit is a 10 uF cap from VCC to the pin and a 10k resistor to ground.",
    "11.0592 MHz is the traditional crystal because it divides exactly into 9600 and the other standard baud rates. 12 MHz gives round instruction timings but ugly baud rates.",
    "The classic 8051 takes 12 clock cycles per machine cycle, so an 11.0592 MHz part runs under a million instructions a second. Single-cycle cores like the STC and the Silicon Labs C8051 are an order of magnitude faster at the same crystal.",
    "The S in AT89S52 means in-system programmable over SPI, using MOSI, MISO and SCK on P1.5, P1.6 and P1.7. The older AT89C52 needs a parallel programmer.",
    "Ports 0 and 2 become the address and data bus if you attach external memory, which leaves you Port 1 and Port 3 for everything else."
  ],
